The distribution of local stress in an atomic system can be calculated from its mechanical definition. While this leads, for a homogeneous system, to an expression equivalent to that derived from the virial theorem (thermodynamic definition), when local inhomogeneities are present the equivalence can be demonstrated only in the sense of a volume average which effectively eliminates the inhomogeneities. Atomistic simulation results are presented to show that for a relaxed crystal with a planar free surface, the normal stress on each atomic plane parallel to the surface, when calculated according to the mechanical definition, vanishes uniformly as expected, whereas if the virial expression were applied the resulting stress distribution shows unphysical oscillations in the surface region.
The macroscopic description of forces acting on a body generally begins with the consideration of stress. At the atomic level one can visualize the determination of local stress through the intermolecular forces arising from interactions among neighboring atoms. For the stress at a given atom, one can set up an element of volume surrounding the atom and calculate the force across each face of the element. This may be called the mechanical approach; conceptually it is a straightforward procedure, though computationally the calculations may be quite tedious. From the virial theorem of Clausius’ an expression for the tensor Q describing the state of stress of a system of particles can be derived which is widely used to calculate the system pressure.’ Because Q can be formally written as a sum over the atoms, each individual term in 0 has been
taken to describe the local stress at an atom.3 Since 4 also can be obtained as the first-order change of the energy with respect to the application of a small, homogeneous strain,’ this may be considered a thermodynamic approach to the formulation of atomic-level stress. With the exception of a study by Tsai5 and a derivation by Lutsko” there appears to be little discussion of the distinction between the mechanical and the virial formulations. In this Communication we consider the determination of the local stress distribution in a system with atomic-level inhomogeneities. By examining highly idealized models we show that the two formulations give different results in the region of inhomogeneities; however, if the distributions are volume averaged over this region, then the formulations are equivalent. By considering molecular dynamics simulations on a relaxed crystal with a free surface we also show that in the surface region the normal stress distribution, on an atomic level, behaves properly when the mechanical delinition is used but not when the virial expression is used. For an N-particle system the virial stress, derived from the virial theorem, is of the formls2
