quenched the liquid to below the freezing point to obtain an amorphous state and found that Eq. (3) gives a stress profile similar to that of the liquid. Thus, structural disorder is apparently sufficient to smooth out the inhomogeneous nature of a free surface. We regard the present work as a supplement to the chain-model analysis of Tsai’ showing that in the presence of a wall the virial and mechanical stress formulations do not agree, and to the statistical-mechanical derivation of Lutsko6 showing that the local stress tensor can be expressed as Eq. (3) provided an averaging over a volume whose radius exceedsthe cutoff of the potential is understood. Taken together, these results imply that while the concept of local stress on a strictly atomic level may not be unique, the mechanical definition based on force acting across a Gibbs dividing surface has the advantage over the virial decomposition, with its thermodynamic origin, in that the former gives physically plausible results in the presence of atomic-level inhomogeneities. Recent applications of this method of calculation have been made in the analysis of dislocation nucleation barrier in crack-tip extension’ and stress relaxation of stepped surfaces on Si (loo)? We thank A. S. Argon and F. F. Abraham for very helpful discussions. This work has been supported by NSF Grants CES-88 15942 and CHE-8806767, with computations carried out at the San Diego Supercomputer Center. K.S.C. also acknowledges the support of an NSF Graduate Fellowship.
